  "body": "My current sketch under \"Contrived API example\" is a little hand-wavey. Here is my vague conception of how it works:\n- You push data with `onRead`'s `push` into an internal buffer.\n- When calling `read`:\n  - If the internal buffer has anything in it, return an immediately-fulfilled promise containing the contents of that internal buffer, and schedule a microtask to clear the buffer.\n  - If the internal buffer is empty, and the stream is not in an \"ended\" state, return a new (pending) promise. When the buffer gets something pushed into it, schedule a microtask to resolve the returned promise with the contents of the internal buffer.\n- If the internal buffer is empty, and the stream is in an \"ended\" state, return an immediately-fulfilled promise for `undefined`.\n\nThis works under the assumption of concatenation (#4). The semantics would be different without that, although with similar interactions.\n\nAn example:\n\n``` js\nconst s = new ReadableStream({ highWaterMark: 1024 }, push => {\n  push(str2ab(\"123\"));\n  setTimeout(() => push(str2ab(\"456\")), 100);\n});\n\ns.read().then(data => console.log(\"call 1 result: \" + ab2str(data));\ns.read().then(data => console.log(\"call 2 result: \" + ab2str(data));\n\nprocess.nextTick(() => {\n  s.read().then(data => console.log(\"call 3 result: \" + ab2str(data));\n});\n```\n\nwill output:\n\n```\ncall 1 result: \"123\"\ncall 2 result: \"123\"\n\n// 100 ms go by...\n\ncall 3 result: \"456\"\n```\n",
